Step 1: Generate your SSH key pair
On your Windows computer, open PowerShell.

Run: ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "geocloud-server"

Part Meaning
ssh-keygen Generates SSH keys.
-t Specifies the key type.
ed25519 The key algorithm.
-C Adds a descriptive comment.

id_ed25519 is your private key.
id_ed25519.pub is your public key.

Public Key is installed on the Ubuntu Machine
On Ubuntu, public keys for a particular user are stored in: ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Once connected, run:
mkdir -p ~/.ssh
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
nano ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Paste it into authorized_keys as one line, save and exit,: chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

700 restricts access to the .ssh directory.
600 restricts access to the authorized keys file.

Now try connecting with your private key: ssh -i "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519" ubuntu@203.0.113.10

You won't need to repeatedly type a server password every time you deploy an update or inspect application logs.

File transfer: SCP and SFTP
Now imagine your GEO_CLOUD project is on your Windows computer, but you need to move it to your Ubuntu server.

SCP — Secure Copy or SFTP — SSH File Transfer Protocol

SCP:
Suppose you have: C:\Users\DELL\Downloads\GEO_CLOUD & You want to copy a ZIP archive to your server.

In Powershell: scp "$env:USERPROFILE\Downloads\GEO_CLOUD.zip" ubuntu@203.0.113.10:/home/ubuntu/ **sample IP number**

The general structure is: scp SOURCE DESTINATION,,, scp local_file.zip user@server:/home/user/

To copy an entire directory recursively, use -r: scp -r ./GEO_CLOUD ubuntu@203.0.113.10:/home/ubuntu/

You can also download something from the server: scp ubuntu@203.0.113.10:/home/ubuntu/app.log . ""the dot means current local directory""

SFTP — SSH File Transfer Protocol

Connect: sftp ubuntu@203.0.113.10
