4. Linux firewall: UFW
   A firewall controls network traffic according to configured rules.

On Ubuntu: sudo ufw status verbose
Port Service Public access?

22 SSH Restricted administrator access
80 HTTP / Nginx Yes
443 HTTPS / Nginx Yes
8000 FastAPI Normally internal only
5432 PostgreSQL Normally internal only

Configure UFW
For our example deployment, we want to permit SSH, HTTP and HTTPS.
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH : This allows the standard SSH service.

sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp

sudo ufw default deny incoming default deny incoming traffic
sudo ufw default allow outgoing default for outgoing

Before enabling UFW, verify that your SSH access is allowed. then

sudo ufw enable
finally
sudo ufw status numbered

**Never enable a restrictive firewall on a remote server without first allowing the SSH connection you're currently using. Otherwise, you can lock yourself out.**

**Linux process management and systemd.**
Each running process has a unique identifier called a PID (Process ID).
How do you see running processes?
On Ubuntu, execute: ps aux

You can filter for your worker: pgrep -af geospatial.worker
You might see something like:
2451 python -m geospatial.worker
Here, 2451 is the PID.

Useful process-management commands
Command Purpose
ps aux List running processes.
pgrep -af python Find Python processes.
top Monitor CPU and memory usage.
free -h Check available memory.
kill PID Request that a process terminate.
kill -9 PID Forcefully terminate a process.

One important distinction:
kill 2451 Normally sends SIGTERM, asking the process to stop gracefully.
Whereas:
kill -9 2451 sends SIGKILL, which forces termination immediately.

You could try:
python -m geospatial.worker &

The & runs the command in the background of your shell.

**systemd** is a system and service manager used by Ubuntu and many other Linux distributions.
Its job includes managing services and other system resources.

A service is a program that is managed by systemd.
For example, your background worker can become a systemd service.

Then you can control it using commands such as:

sudo systemctl start geocloud-worker
sudo systemctl stop geocloud-worker
sudo systemctl restart geocloud-worker
sudo systemctl status geocloud-worker

You no longer need to manually locate the Python process and manage it yourself.

Create a service file: sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/geocloud-worker.service
The .service extension identifies a systemd service unit.

Step 2: Understand the service configuration
Add this:
[Unit]
Description=GEO_CLOUD Background Worker
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple

User=ubuntu
WorkingDirectory=/opt/geocloud

EnvironmentFile=/etc/geocloud/worker.env

ExecStart=/opt/geocloud/.venv/bin/python -m geospatial.worker

Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

KillSignal=SIGTERM
TimeoutStopSec=120

StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target





"""
Description gives the service a human-readable name.
After establishes startup ordering relative to another systemd target.
Wants requests that the network-online target also be started.

Type=simple    This tells systemd that the service is a continuously running process.
User=ubuntu    The worker runs as the Ubuntu user rather than as root.



Create the configuration directory: sudo mkdir -p /etc/geocloud


Create the environment file:   sudo nano /etc/geocloud/worker.env


An example might look like:
GEO_CLOUD_ENV=production
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/gis_jobs
GEO_CLOUD_DATASETS_DIR=/var/lib/geocloud/datasets


These are illustrative values. Configure the actual production credentials, database address and storage location for your deployment.

Set appropriate permissions:   sudo chmod 600 /etc/geocloud/worker.env

Once the service file has been created, execute:   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
It tells systemd to reload its service definitions because we have introduced a new service file.

sudo systemctl start geocloud-worker
then: sudo systemctl status geocloud-worker

Now we want to make it start automatically when Ubuntu boots: sudo systemctl enable --now geocloud-worker

